<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Thermopylae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/480-bce">480 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Thermopylae · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Thermopylae was fought in 480 BCE at a narrow coastal pass in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, where a small Greek force led by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas of Sparta</a> held off the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> for several days. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> eventually outflanked the defenders and destroyed them. The battle allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> to advance into central Greece, but it became an enduring symbol of resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae.jpg" alt="Battle of Thermopylae" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Louis David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9onidas_aux_Thermopyles_-_Jacques-Louis_David_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_Peintures_INV_3690_;_L_3711.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria had aided the Ionian Revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> in 499–494 BCE, and Darius vowed to punish them. His first invasion of Greece ended in 490 BCE with the Greek victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a>. A decade later his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> had assembled a very large land and naval force to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> proposed that the allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> block the Persian army at the pass of Thermopylae while the fleet held the Straits of Artemisium. The Spartans sent an advance force under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a>, with the 300 men of the royal bodyguard, to block the pass.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Greek force numbered approximately 7,000. Ancient authors gave the Persian army in the millions; modern scholars estimate between 120,000 and 300,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> attacked on the fifth day after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> reached the pass, first with 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, whose arrows the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>' shields and bronze helmets deflected, then with waves of about 10,000 men. The Greeks fought at the narrowest part of the pass in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> of overlapping shields, and the Persians could make no headway on the first day or the second.</p><p>On the second day Ephialtes, a local man motivated by the desire for a reward, told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> of a mountain path around the pass. His name came to mean nightmare in Greek and to stand for the archetypal traitor. On the third day a Persian column bypassed the Phocians guarding the path. Leonidas, aware that he was being outflanked, dismissed most of the Greek army and stayed to guard the retreat with 300 Spartans and 700 Thespians; up to 900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> and 400 Thebans are also reported to have remained. Most of the Thebans reportedly surrendered, and the others fought to the death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> now held the pass, and the Greek fleet at Artemisium withdrew to Salamis. The Persians overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> and captured the evacuated city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, but the Greek fleet defeated them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Battle of Salamis</a> in late 480 BCE. Xerxes withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> with much of his army, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a>, who was decisively defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a> the following year. Writers ancient and modern have used Thermopylae as an example of an army defending its native soil.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Thermopylae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its campaigns included Cyrus's conquest of Lydia, where camels were reportedly deployed to unsettle the Lydian cavalry, the conquest of Egypt (525 BCE), Darius's Scythian campaign (c. 513 BCE), and Xerxes's massive invasion of Greece (480 BCE), which brought victory at <b>Thermopylae</b> and the sack of Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Each people sent two delegates, the hieromnemones, to meetings held each spring and autumn at Delphi and at Anthela near <b>Thermopylae</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Thucydides reports that his host was said to number about 150,000 men, a third of them cavalry; the figure is probably inflated, but the army alarmed the Greeks as far south as <b>Thermopylae</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 480 BCE Leonidas I held the pass of <b>Thermopylae</b> with a small Greek force including 300 Spartiates until he was outflanked and killed, and in 479 BCE the regent Pausanias commanded the Greek army that destroyed the Persian land forces at the Battle of Plataea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 506 BCE the Thebans repelled an inroad into Attica; in 480 BCE they sent 400 men to <b>Thermopylae</b>, who were defeated alongside the Spartans, and then fought for Xerxes I at the Battle of Plataea in 479 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The singer recalls learning as a boy of the ancient fighters for freedom, the Spartans at <b>Thermopylae</b> and the Horatii, and dreams of the day when Ireland will be free.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-nation-once-again">A Nation Once Again</a></li></ul></section>
